    "q-332744": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332744",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|1-0.4+\\frac{(0.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{419}{625}| \\\\  & =0.670\\,4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|1-1+\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3}{8}| \\\\  & =0.375<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2)| & =|1-2+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =0.333…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable.",
