    "q-333544": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333544",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$",
      "worked_solution": "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+4{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+4Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+4Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.1.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".",
